\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Matching traces on a finite adjoint-closed algebra}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let #{\mathscr A} be a unital adjoint-closed complex algebra
of operators on a nonzero finite-dimensional Hermitian space.
Let #{\pi_1,\pi_2} be two unital complex-linear representations
on finite-dimensional complex Hermitian spaces, preserving
adjoints. They are unitarily equivalent exactly when
##{\trace(\pi_1(x))=\trace(\pi_2(x))\qquad(x\in\mathscr A).}}

\proof{
\p{Take the orthogonal projections of \ref{fdip-018V}. In
each equivalence class choose a representative #{p_r}.
For #{p_i} in the class choose #{v_i\in p_i\mathscr A p_r}
with initial projection #{p_r} and final projection #{p_i}.
Take #{v_r=p_r}. Then #{e_{ij}=v_i v_j^\dagger} has adjoint
#{e_{ji}}. Within the class,
#{e_{ij}e_{kl}=\delta_{jk}e_{il}} because the ranges of
different #{v_j} are orthogonal and #{v_j^\dagger v_j=p_r}.
Products between classes vanish. Every #{x\in\mathscr A} is
the sum of its corners #{p_i x p_j}. Within a class the
nonzero #{e_{ij}} spans this corner space, and the corner is
zero between classes. Thus these matrix units span the algebra.}

\p{In either representation the images #{\pi_s(p_i)} give an
orthogonal decomposition of the representation space, allowing
zero summands. Within a class, #{\pi_s(v_i)} identifies the
range of #{\pi_s(p_r)} isometrically with that of #{\pi_s(p_i)}.
Trace equality on #{p_r} says the two representative ranges
have the same dimension, since an orthogonal projection has
trace equal to its rank. Choose a unitary #{b_r} from the first
representative range to the second, including the unique map
between zero spaces. On the range of #{\pi_1(p_i)} use
##{b_i=\pi_2(v_i)b_r\pi_1(v_i)^\dagger.}
These isometries give an onto unitary on the orthogonal sum.
For each #{e_{ij}}, both compositions on the #{j}th
range equal #{\pi_2(v_i)b_r\pi_1(v_j)^\dagger}, and both are
zero on all other ranges. Linearity supplies intertwining on
every element. Conversely, unitary conjugation preserves trace.}
}

\p{TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{IsSemisimpleModule.nonempty_linearEquiv_of_trace_eq}
obtains a linear module equivalence from equality of all action
traces for finite-dimensional semisimple modules over an algebra
in characteristic zero. For the complex representations here,
the orthogonal complement of each invariant subspace is
invariant because adjoints are in the action. Splitting by
dimension gives semisimplicity. The range identifications
above preserve the supplied Hermitian metrics.}
